    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3ee145477967f52695770fc62a4efaa501caa4f3dd308d6535aebd80b83dbb97",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The triangular, natural, and smoothed constructions all separate local coordinate correctness from global Hilbert-norm control. Boundary-corrected coefficients make the first n errors vanish exactly, while the natural and smoothed coefficients converge to the target in each fixed coordinate; each construction also controls sufficiently far-out coordinates. What remains is possible norm mass in a moving finite or low-frequency block, so a common square-summable dominator on that block is the additional burden rather than a consequence of coordinatewise convergence.",
